# Materials, Matter & History Taste & Wonder Cache
#
# Material memory of civilization, tactile reality vs digital abstraction, and the ethics of material extraction.

#>spw_cache_mat_hist_taste
#:cache #!materials_matter_and_history_taste
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"intent"{
  ~#goal: "Articulate the tactile awe, historical gravity, and philosophical reverence demanded by the material substances that construct human civilization."
  ~#protocol: "cache.layer/1"
}

^"taste_principles"{
  "civilization_as_sculpted_matter": .{
    the_physicality_of_thought: "Human culture is not an ethereal cloud of disembodied ideas; every symphony, legal constitution, and scientific proof exists because physical matter was disciplined into meaning—ink soaking into flax fibers, iron hammered upon anvils, pigments binding in drying oil, and clean water quenching human thirst",
    the_tactile_antidote_to_the_virtual: "In an era of frictionless digital screens and disembodied software, touching raw matter—feeling the cold heft of forged steel, smelling the linseed oil curing on canvas, watching ink spread by capillary suction through rag paper—grounds human consciousness in tactile reality",
  }[reg=facet],
  "the_moral_dialectic_of_materials": .{
    the_blood_beneath_the_substance: "No material is innocent; materials that liberated human freedom often carried terrifying historical brutality: the spinning jenny and cotton gin entrenching chattel slavery across an entire continent; Bessemer steel fueling the mass butchery of industrialized trench warfare; synthetic petrochemical fibers choking the oceans with microplastics",
    fiduciary_material_stewardship: "To understand materials science is to embrace ethical responsibility: designing circular, non-toxic, regenerative material cycles that honor the finite biosphere from which every atom was borrowed",
  }[reg=facet],
  "the_eternal_poetry_of_the_elemental": .{
    the_common_thread: "A continuous, unbroken human lineage connects the Paleolithic child blowing red iron ochre around their mother's hand on a cold cave wall in Chauvet to the contemporary engineer 3D-printing a vascularized cardiac organoid or welding the steel hull of an interplanetary rocket",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"wonder_probes"{
  #>wonder_mat_hist_taste_1
  ?["Why did the humble binary punch card—invented in 1804 by a French silk weaver to create floral damask tapestries—become the physical foundation of modern digital computing?"]{
    #:depth #!philosophical ~#lens(textile_mechanics_as_the_cradle_of_software)
    ~#hypothesis: "Weaving is inherently binary: at every thread intersection, the warp yarn is either raised (1) or lowered (0) over the weft pick. Joseph Marie Jacquard recognized that this physical binary state could be abstracted into hole-or-no-hole on cardboard strips. Charles Babbage, Ada Lovelace, and Herman Hollerith saw that Jacquard's cards did not just store decorative pictures; they stored algorithmic instructions, proving that computation was born not from mathematics, but from the mechanical looms of textile workers."
    !probe{ "Trace the architectural evolution of binary state representation from Jacquard loom prism readers through Hollerith census tabulators to ENIAC programming cards." }
    $%[computation.binary_state_loom_origin_correlation, history.punch_card_information_density_bytes_cm2]
  }

  #>wonder_mat_hist_taste_2
  ?["How did the invention of the collapsible metal paint tube in 1841 fundamentally shift the entire history of Western art from academic neoclassical portraits to French Impressionism?"]{
    #:depth #!historical ~#lens(material_portability_and_aesthetic_paradigm_shifts)
    ~#hypothesis: "Before the tin tube, wet oil paint dried in minutes once exposed to air and had to be freshly ground by hand daily and stored in fragile pig bladders. Painters were physically chained to indoor academic studios under artificial light. The screw-cap tin tube preserved wet oil paint for months, allowing painters to step outdoors onto trains, beaches, and city boulevards, capturing the fleeting, changing sensations of natural sunlight that defined the Impressionist revolution."
    !probe{ "Audit the percentage of plein air exterior canvases produced in Western painting before 1841 vs after 1870 across major museum collections." }
    $%[art_history.plein_air_canvas_percentage_lift, materials.paint_tube_shelf_life_months]
  }
}
